{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a3f7aae6f3877e54", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t2026 IEEE INTERNATIONAL WORKSHOP ON MACHINE LEARNING FOR SIGNAL PROCESSING, SEP. 28– OCT. 1, 2026, ATLANTA, USA\n2\t\n3\tDYNAMIC CLUSTERING FOR CROSS-SEGMENT PERMUTATION ALIGNMENT\n4\tIN LONG SPEECH SEPARATION\n5\tYuzhu Wang1 , Archontis Politis1 , Konstantinos Drossos2 , Tuomas Virtanen1\n6\t1\n7\t\n8\tSignal Processing Research Centre, Tampere University, Tampere, Finland\n9\t2\n10\tNokia Technologies, Espoo, Finland\n11\t\n12\tarXiv:2608.09451v1 [eess.AS] 10 Aug 2026\n13\t\n14\tABSTRACT\n15\tLong speech separation typically employs a segment-separationstitch paradigm where recordings are divided into short segments,\n16\tprocessed independently, and stitched together. Its challenge lies\n17\tin predicting cross-segment permutations. This paper proposes a\n18\ttraining-free dynamic clustering approach for cross-segment permutation alignment using speaker embedding reference pools. The\n19\tmethod predicts the permutation using the cosine similarity between\n20\tcurrent segment embeddings and the reference pools. The approach\n21\tupdates reference pools by retaining the most representative speaker\n22\tembeddings based on their overall cosine similarity with existing\n23\treferences. As a plug-and-play post-processing module compatible\n24\twith existing separation models, the proposed method demonstrates\n25\tsuperior performance compared to existing methods on dense and\n26\tsparse long speech scenarios, particularly in challenging sparse scenarios with extended utterance gaps, and further shows robustness to\n27\tspeaker count estimation errors in unknown speaker count scenarios.\n28\tIndex Terms— Speech separation, permutation, clustering,\n29\tspeaker embeddings, long speech processing\n30\t1. INTRODUCTION\n31\tSpeech separation is the task of isolating individual speaker signals\n32\tfrom mixtures. Deep neural network (DNN) based methods have\n33\tbecome mainstream [1–3]. Most current research focuses on separation models operating on short segments, typically ranging from\n34\ta few seconds to tens of seconds [4–7]. However, practical applications often involve arbitrarily long recordings or continuously\n35\tstreamed audio such as meetings, lectures, and broadcasts.\n36\tSeparating arbitrarily long recordings can be achieved by enhancing the generalization capability of separation models [8–11],\n37\tbut such methods typically generalize only to recording lengths of\n38\tseveral minutes before facing computational complexity explosion\n39\tand performance degradation. A more scalable alternative involves\n40\tsegmenting long recordings into shorter segments during inference,\n41\tperforming separation on each segment independently, and then\n42\tstitching the results into complete outputs [12–15]. Theoretically,\n43\tthis paradigm can handle arbitrarily long recordings. Since separation models are typically speaker-independent and trained using\n44\tpermutation invariant training (PIT) [5], the assignment of speakers to outputs is arbitrary and inconsistent between segments, and\n45\tthe performance of such approaches is therefore dominated by the\n46\taccuracy of cross-segment permutation assignment.\n47\tExisting solutions to the cross-segment permutation problem\n48\tcan be categorized into three types. The first approach is overlap matching, as used in continuous speech separation (CSS) [12],\n49\twhich determines the permutation of each segment by matching the\n50\t\n51\toverlapping region between adjacent segments. This approach is\n52\tcomputationally efficient and requires no speaker embeddings, but\n53\tonly ensures local utterance continuity within each output stream\n54\twithout resolving the global permutation across the full recording. The second approach applies global clustering to speaker\n55\tembeddings extracted from separated outputs [13]. The standard\n56\tpipeline extracts speaker embeddings from voice activity detection\n57\t(VAD)-segmented speech regions and groups them using clustering\n58\talgorithms such as k-means, spectral clustering, or agglomerative\n59\tclustering, assigning speaker labels globally across all segments.\n60\tHowever, global clustering is limited to offline processing. Residual\n61\tinterference in separated outputs increases the risk of VAD errors\n62\tthat introduce unreliable embeddings, which can distort clustering results by shifting centroids or causing misassignment across\n63\tspeaker clusters, with the severity depending on the robustness of\n64\tthe chosen clustering algorithm [14]. The third approach maintains\n65\ta speaker cache to guide permutation alignment. Static cache methods, such as speaker inventory [15], pre-detect single-active-speaker\n66\tregions from the mixture to build a fixed speaker reference that is\n67\tused without further updates. Dynamic speaker cache methods,\n68\tas employed in streaming speaker diarization such as the speakertracing buffer (STB) [16] and more recently arrival-order speaker\n69\tcache (AOSC) [17], maintain a set of continuously updated speaker\n70\trepresentations for streaming identity tracking, proven effective in\n71\tspeaker diarization.\n72\tAll three approaches are used in meeting processing systems\n73\twhere the primary goal is automatic speech recognition (ASR) and\n74\ttranscription. In such systems, cross-segment permutation consistency receives limited attention, as individual utterances are recognized independently after VAD segmentation, and utterance-level\n75\talignment is sufficient to produce accurate recognition results. In\n76\tcontrast, human listening applications such as hearing aids, multiparty call recording, and interview production require consistent\n77\tspeaker identity across multiple utterances. Permutation errors\n78\tcause different speakers’ voices to alternate within the same output\n79\tchannel, severely degrading the perceptual quality. Dedicated study\n80\tof cross-segment permutation alignment for separated audio is lacking, particularly for sparse speech scenarios with extended utterance\n81\tgaps.\n82\tTo address the cross-segment permutation problem in long\n83\tspeech separation, we propose a dynamic clustering approach built\n84\tupon the speaker cache strategy, as shown in Fig. 1. Similar to\n85\tdynamic cache methods in streaming speaker diarization [16, 17],\n86\tthe proposed method maintains a reference pool for each speaker\n87\tand resolves cross-segment permutations by matching current segment embeddings against cached historical representations. However, speaker diarization systems typically operate on mixture signals and output temporal speaker activity labels targeting ASR\n88\t\n89\t\fpipelines [18, 19], whereas the proposed method operates directly\n90\ton separated outputs to produce speaker-consistent audio streams.\n91\tFurthermore, recent dynamic cache methods rely on neural network\n92\tprediction scores for cache updates [16, 17], making their generalization performance dependent on the match between training data\n93\tand the target scenario. In contrast, since separation quality varies\n94\tacross segments and low-quality embeddings from poorly separated\n95\tregions are unavoidable, the proposed method measures embedding reliability through inter-embedding weighted cosine similarity\n96\tand retains only the most representative embeddings in the reference pool via a TopN update strategy, naturally suppressing lowquality embeddings without relying on VAD. The proposed method\n97\trequires no additional training and functions as a plug-and-play\n98\tpost-processing module compatible with existing separation models. Evaluation on dense and sparse long speech scenarios shows\n99\tthat the proposed method achieves robust performance compared to\n100\texisting approaches, with particularly substantial improvements in\n101\tsparse scenarios. The proposed method also exhibits robustness to\n102\tspeaker count estimation errors, maintaining superior performance\n103\tover baselines even when the speaker count is incorrectly estimated.\n104\t2. PROBLEM FORMULATION\n105\tP\n106\tConsider a long-duration mixture signal y(t) = C\n107\tc=1 xc (t) + n(t)\n108\tcontaining speech from C unknown speakers and noise n(t), where\n109\txc (t) consists of multiple utterances separated by silence intervals. The mixture is divided into S segments of length L with\n110\thop size H (0 < H ≤ L). Each segment ys (t) is processed\n111\tby a speaker-independent separation model to obtain C outputs\n112\t{x̂s,1 (t), . . . , x̂s,C (t)}. Since speaker identities are unknown, the\n113\tassignment of speakers and separated outputs is arbitrary and inconsistent between segments. The unknown permutation for segment s\n114\tis denoted as πs ∈ ΠC , where ΠC denotes the set of all permutations of {1, 2, . . . , C}, and πs (c) indicates which speaker the c-th\n115\tseparated output corresponds to. The cross-segment permutation\n116\tproblem aims to predict the sequence of permutations {π1∗ , . . . , πS∗ }\n117\tsuch that separated segments from the same speaker are consistently\n118\tassigned to the same output index across all segments. The number\n119\tof active speakers and utterances in each segment ys (t) is unknown\n120\tand variable.\n121\t3. PROPOSED METHOD\n122\tSeparation quality varies across segments for long recordings, and\n123\tsegments where speakers are insufficiently separated or where residual noise is prominent easily yield low-quality embeddings. As illustrated in Fig. 1, the proposed method maintains a separate reference pool for each speaker and retains only the most representative\n124\tembeddings via a TopN selection strategy. Since high cosine similarity scores can only arise from embeddings belonging to the same\n125\tspeaker, the reference pool naturally converges toward high-quality\n126\tsame-speaker embeddings over time while progressively displacing\n127\tunreliable ones, remaining robust to noise and capable of accumulating representative embeddings across arbitrarily long recordings.\n128\t\n129\tFig. 1: Dynamic clustering for cross-segment permutation: (a) long\n130\tspeech separation workflow, (b) quality-weighted permutation assignment using reference pools, (c) reference pool updates based on\n131\tsegment quality assessment.\n132\tat least one active speaker in the first N segments. This assumption is easily satisfied in practice, as leading silence regions can\n133\tbe removed by simple preprocessing before processing. For the\n134\tfirst segment, we use identity permutation where π1 (c) = c for all\n135\tc, and the embeddings {e1,1 , . . . , e1,C } are added to their corresponding reference pools with quality score initialized to 0.5. For\n136\teach segment s > 1, the permutation is determined by finding the\n137\tassignment that maximizes the quality-weighted similarity between\n138\tcurrent separated output embeddings and reference embeddings in\n139\tthe reference pools,\n140\tπs∗ = arg max\n141\t\n142\tπ∈ΠC\n143\t\n144\tC\n145\tX\n146\t\n147\tX\n148\t\n149\tqj · sim(es,π(c) , eref\n150\tj ),\n151\t\n152\t(1)\n153\t\n154\tc=1 (eref ,qj )∈Rs−1,c\n155\tj\n156\t\n157\twhere sim(·, ·) denotes cosine similarity, which is widely used for\n158\tcomparing speaker embeddings due to its effectiveness in capturing angular relationships between high-dimensional vectors [20,21].\n159\tThe quality scores qj are used to emphasize representative embeddings over less characteristic ones in Eq. (1).\n160\t3.2. Quality Assessment\n161\tThe quality score qs for segment s is computed as the weighted cosine similarity between all speakers’ embeddings and their corresponding reference pools as\n162\tqs =\n163\t\n164\tC\n165\tX\n166\t1\n167\tC · |Rs−1,c | c=1\n168\t\n169\tX\n170\t\n171\tqj · sim(e∗s,c , eref\n172\tj ),\n173\t\n174\t(2)\n175\t\n176\t(eref\n177\tj ,qj )∈Rs−1,c\n178\t\n179\t3.1. Quality-Driven Dynamic Clustering\n180\tWe extract speaker embeddings {es,1 , . . . , es,C } from separated\n181\toutputs of segment s using a pretrained speaker embedding model,\n182\tas shown in Fig. 1(b). For each segment s, there are C separate\n183\tdynamic reference pools Rs,c , where each pool contains up to N\n184\tref\n185\ttuples (eref\n186\tj , qj ) of embeddings ej and their corresponding quality\n187\tscores qj from previously processed segments. The method assumes\n188\t\n189\twhere e∗s,c is the embedding extracted from the separated output assigned to speaker c using predicted πs∗ from Eq. (1).\n190\t3.3. Reference Pool Management\n191\tAs shown in Fig. 1(c), the reference pools are updated by retaining\n192\tthe top N highest-quality embeddings from the union of the current\n193\t\n194\t\fembeddings and existing reference pool embeddings,\n195\t(\n196\tRs−1,c ∪ {(e∗s,c , qs )}\n197\tif |Rs−1,c | < N\n198\tRs,c =\n199\tTopN(Rs−1,c ∪ {(e∗s,c , qs )}) otherwise\n200\t\n201\t(3)\n202\t\n203\twhere N is the maximum pool size, the quality score qs is from\n204\tEq. (2), and TopN(·) selects the N highest-score embeddings.\n205\t4. EXPERIMENTAL SETTINGS\n206\t4.1. Datasets\n207\tWe generate long speech test sets with 2 to 4 speakers to evaluate permutation alignment under varying conditions. The 2-speaker\n208\tdense and sparse test sets follow the generation approach in [10]. For\n209\teach mixture, we randomly select two speakers from LibriSpeech\n210\ttrain-clean-360 [22] and concatenate all utterances for each speaker\n211\tin random order. We use train-clean-360 rather than test-clean due\n212\tto the limited number of speakers and fewer utterances per speaker\n213\tin test-clean, which are insufficient for generating long speech test\n214\tsets. Random silence intervals are inserted before each utterance,\n215\twith durations uniformly sampled from [1, 10] seconds for the dense\n216\ttest set and [10, 30] seconds for the sparse test set. The overlap rate\n217\tis defined as the ratio of overlapping speech duration to the total\n218\tduration containing active speech. Room acoustic simulation and\n219\treal-world noise addition follow the parameters as [10]. All signals\n220\tare single-channel at 16 kHz sampling rate with no speaker overlap\n221\tbetween training and test sets. Each test set contains 200 mixtures.\n222\tThe dense test set achieves overlap rates from 31.2% to 69.7% with\n223\tan average of 50.1%, resulting in a total duration of 121 hours with\n224\tmixture lengths averaging 36 minutes (range: 34-38 minutes). The\n225\tsparse test set achieves overlap rates from 8.5% to 29.4% with an\n226\taverage of 19.8%, resulting in a total duration of 213 hours with\n227\tmixture lengths averaging 64 minutes (range: 54-76 minutes).\n228\tWe generate 3-speaker and 4-speaker sparse long speech test sets\n229\tfollowing the same simulation procedure and parameter settings as\n230\tthe 2-speaker case, with at most 3 speakers active simultaneously,\n231\tfollowing the setup in [12].\n232\t4.2. Evaluation Metrics\n233\tWe evaluate the permutation solutions using SI-SDR [23] and\n234\tsegment-wise permutation accuracy. SI-SDR is computed on the\n235\tentire long speech signal after stitching all segments, reflecting both\n236\twithin-segment separation quality and cross-segment permutation\n237\tconsistency. We determine oracle permutations for each segment\n238\tbetween separated outputs x̂ and ground truth reference signals xref\n239\tas\n240\tC\n241\tX\n242\tπsoracle = arg min\n243\tLSI-SDR (x̂s,π(c) , xref\n244\t(4)\n245\ts,c ).\n246\tπ∈ΠC\n247\t\n248\tc=1\n249\t\n250\tFrom the accuracy calculation, we exclude segments where all reference signals are silent using a root mean square (RMS) energy\n251\tthreshold of −30 dB, as speaker permutations are undefined in these\n252\tsegments. The segment-wise permutation accuracy is computed as\n253\tX\n254\t1\n255\t1[πs∗ = πsoracle ],\n256\t(5)\n257\tAccuracy =\n258\t|Svalid | s∈S\n259\tvalid\n260\t\n261\twhere Svalid denotes the set of non-silent segments, πs∗ is the predicted permutation, and 1[·] is the indicator function that equals 1\n262\twhen the condition is true and 0 otherwise. To account for potential global speaker assignment ambiguity, we evaluate all possible\n263\tglobal permutations of speaker assignments and select the one with\n264\tthe highest accuracy.\n265\t\n266\tTable 1: Performance comparison of different systems using 8 s\n267\tsegments with 25% overlap. Dense scenario: overlap rate 50.1%,\n268\tunprocessed mixture SI-SDR −1.4 dB. Sparse scenario: overlap\n269\trate 19.8%, unprocessed mixture SI-SDR −1.2 dB. Values show\n270\tsegment-wise permutation accuracy(%) / SI-SDR(dB).\n271\tSystem\n272\t\n273\tDense\n274\t\n275\tSparse\n276\t\n277\tOracle\n278\tCSS-sep [12]\n279\tCSS-sep + VAD + emb. + k-clustering [14]\n280\tSep + speaker inventory [15]\n281\tSortformer (AOSC) [17]\n282\tSep + VAD + emb. + UPGMC-clustering [13]\n283\t\n284\t–/11.8\n285\t59.1/-3.0\n286\t97.1/11.2\n287\t85.0/5.7\n288\t85.2/5.9\n289\t91.3/9.2\n290\t\n291\t–/14.3\n292\t53.7/-4.5\n293\t89.3/8.2\n294\t74.1/3.5\n295\t71.1/2.7\n296\t71.6/2.8\n297\t\n298\tSep + emb. + dynamic clustering (proposed)\n299\t\n300\t98.5/11.7\n301\t\n302\t96.8/13.3\n303\t\n304\t4.3. Baselines and Pretrained Models\n305\tWe compare the proposed method against five baselines. CSSsep [12] stitches separated segments by matching overlapping regions between adjacent segments. CSS-sep + VAD + embedding +\n306\tk-clustering [14] applies VAD to CSS outputs, extracts speaker embeddings from speech-active regions, and reassigns output streams\n307\tvia global k-means clustering. Sep + speaker inventory [15] identifies single-speaker regions to build a fixed speaker reference,\n308\twhich is established once and unchanged throughout the recording\n309\tfor permutation guidance. Sep + VAD + embedding + UPGMC\n310\tclustering [13] extracts speaker embeddings from VAD-segmented\n311\tspeech regions of each separated segment and groups them using\n312\tan agglomerative hierarchical clustering with centroid linkage (UPGMC algorithm). Sortformer (AOSC) [17] is an end-to-end online\n313\tneural diarization system. We use the released model1 and run it\n314\ton the mixture to obtain global speaker diarization labels, which\n315\tare then converted to segment-wise permutations for cross-segment\n316\talignment of the separated outputs following [24]. The model was\n317\ttrained on 2445 hours of real conversations and 5150 hours of simulated mixtures including LibriSpeech, which potentially overlaps\n318\twith the test sets used in this work.\n319\tAll methods use the same FTRNN separator trained following the original implementation [10], with a 2-output version for\n320\t2-speaker experiments and a 3-output version for 3-speaker and 4speaker experiments. Methods requiring VAD employ a pre-trained\n321\tpyannote voice activity detection model2 [25]. For methods requiring speaker embeddings, we employ a pre-trained ECAPA-TDNN\n322\tmodel3 [21] trained on VoxCeleb [26]. Speaker inventory employs\n323\ta pre-trained speaker diarization model4 [13] to identify singlespeaker segments. All pretrained models operate in a zero-shot\n324\tmanner, as their training data does not overlap with the separation\n325\tmodel’s training data. Dynamic clustering uses a reference pool size\n326\tof N = 10, where the sensitivity to this parameter is examined in\n327\tthe ablation study.\n328\t5. RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS\n329\t5.1. Overall Performance\n330\tTable 1 presents the overall performance of all methods on the 2speaker dense and sparse long speech scenarios. CSS-sep achieves\n331\t59.1% and 53.7% permutation accuracy on the dense and sparse\n332\tscenarios respectively. It is worth noting that CSS is not designed\n333\t1 huggingface.co/nvidia/diar streaming sortformer 4spk-v2\n334\t2 huggingface.co/pyannote/voice-activity-detection\n335\t3 huggingface.co/speechbrain/spkrec-ecapa-voxceleb\n336\t4 huggingface.co/pyannote/speaker-diarization-3.1\n337\t\n338\t\fFig. 2: Permutation accuracy on the sparse long speech scenario as a function of (a) segment length (segment overlap=25%, 2 speakers), (b)\n339\tsegment overlap (segment length=8 s, 2 speakers), and (c) number of speakers (segment length=8 s, segment overlap=25%).\n340\tfor global permutation alignment but rather aims to minimize overlap in output streams. CSS-sep + VAD + embedding + k-clustering\n341\tperforms substantially better in the dense scenario (97.1%), building on the local utterance continuity provided by CSS. In the sparse\n342\tscenario, performance drops to 89.3%, as the CSS stitching stage becomes less reliable under longer utterance gaps and the higher proportion of silence increases the risk of VAD false detections. Sep\n343\t+ speaker inventory achieves 85.0% and 74.1% on the dense and\n344\tsparse scenarios, showing consistent but moderate performance in\n345\tboth cases. Sortformer (AOSC) achieves 85.2% and 71.1% on the\n346\tdense and sparse scenarios respectively. Despite potential speaker\n347\toverlap between its training data and our test sets, the performance\n348\tremains moderate, suggesting that the generalization of end-to-end\n349\tdiarization systems is sensitive to mismatches in acoustic conditions such as noise, reverberation, and overlap rate between training\n350\tand test scenarios. Sep + VAD + embedding + UPGMC clustering\n351\tachieves 91.3% in the dense scenario, lower than CSS + VAD + embedding + k-clustering. In the sparse scenario, performance drops\n352\tmore severely to 71.6%, possibly due to increased VAD false detections under high silence proportions, which introduces unreliable\n353\tembeddings into the clustering process.\n354\tThe proposed method achieves 98.5%/11.7 dB in the dense scenario and 96.8%/13.3 dB in the sparse scenario, respectively. The\n355\tSI-SDR of 11.7 dB in the dense scenario is very close to the oracle\n356\tof 11.8 dB, indicating that the proposed method approaches the upper bound of achievable performance. The higher oracle SI-SDR in\n357\tthe sparse scenario (14.3 dB vs. 11.8 dB) reflects that lower overlap\n358\trates make within-segment separation inherently easier. Across all\n359\tmethods, the larger performance gap from oracle in the sparse scenario confirms that permutation accuracy is the primary bottleneck.\n360\t\n361\t5.2. Analysis on Sparse Scenario\n362\tAs shown in Fig. 2(a), all methods show relatively stable performance across segment lengths in the 2-speaker sparse scenario, with\n363\tminor improvements for longer segments. As shown in Fig. 2(b),\n364\tCSS-sep and CSS-sep + VAD + embedding + k-clustering require\n365\tnon-zero overlap and are therefore not applicable at 0% overlap,\n366\twhile the remaining methods show stable performance across overlap rates. As shown in Fig. 2(c), all methods experience performance\n367\tdegradation when the number of speakers increases from 2 to 3,\n368\treflecting the increased permutation complexity with more speakers. CSS-sep shows the most severe degradation, approaching near-\n369\t\n370\tTable 2: Ablation study on dynamic clustering components using\n371\t8 s segments with 25% overlap in sparse scenario. Values show\n372\tsegment-wise permutation accuracy(%)/SI-SDR(dB).\n373\t#\n374\t\n375\tQuality Weighting\n376\tin Eq. (1)\n377\t\n378\tQuality Weighting\n379\tin Eq. (2)\n380\t\n381\tSimilarity\n382\t\n383\tN\n384\t\n385\tInit.\n386\tScore\n387\t\n388\tAcc/SI-SDR\n389\t\n390\t1\n391\t2\n392\t3\n393\t4\n394\t\n395\t✓\n396\t×\n397\t✓\n398\t×\n399\t\n400\t✓\n401\t✓\n402\t×\n403\t×\n404\t\n405\tCosine\n406\tCosine\n407\tCosine\n408\tCosine\n409\t\n410\t10\n411\t10\n412\t10\n413\t10\n414\t\n415\t0.5\n416\t0.5\n417\t0.5\n418\t0.5\n419\t\n420\t96.8/13.3\n421\t95.8/12.7\n422\t90.8/10.6\n423\t90.6/10.5\n424\t\n425\t5\n426\t6\n427\t\n428\t✓\n429\t✓\n430\t\n431\t✓\n432\t✓\n433\t\n434\tCosine\n435\tCosine\n436\t\n437\t5\n438\t2\n439\t\n440\t0.5\n441\t0.5\n442\t\n443\t96.3/13.0\n444\t96.3/12.9\n445\t\n446\t7\n447\t8\n448\t\n449\t✓\n450\t✓\n451\t\n452\t✓\n453\t✓\n454\t\n455\tCosine\n456\tCosine\n457\t\n458\t10\n459\t10\n460\t\n461\t0.25\n462\t0.75\n463\t\n464\t96.8/13.3\n465\t96.8/13.3\n466\t\n467\t9\n468\t\n469\t✓\n470\t\n471\t✓\n472\t\n473\tEuclidean\n474\t\n475\t10\n476\t\n477\t0.5\n478\t\n479\t68.2/1.5\n480\t\n481\trandom performance. The proposed method achieves the smallest\n482\trelative degradation and maintains the highest accuracy among all\n483\tmethods. For scenarios with more speakers, the separator output\n484\tcount is typically determined by the maximum number of simultaneously active speakers rather than the total speaker count [12], and\n485\tresults under such settings are evaluated in Section 5.4.\n486\t5.3. Ablation Study\n487\tTable 2 presents an ablation study on the 2-speaker sparse scenario\n488\texamining the contribution of each component in dynamic clustering. Experiments 1-4 demonstrate the importance of quality weighting mechanisms. Removing quality weighting from permutation\n489\tdecisions (experiment 2) reduces accuracy by 1.0%, while removing quality weighting in pool updates (experiment 3) causes severe\n490\tdegradation of 6.0%. The method exhibits robustness to various parameter settings. Reference pool size variations (experiments 5-6)\n491\tshow minimal performance impact, with N = 5 and N = 2 achieving comparable results to N = 10. Initial quality score settings\n492\t(experiments 7-8) have negligible effect on final performance. Similarity metric choice proves critical, as replacing cosine similarity\n493\twith Euclidean distance (experiment 9) causes severe performance\n494\tdegradation to 68.2% accuracy.\n495\t5.4. Robustness to Speaker Count Mismatch\n496\tAll experiments above assume the number of speakers C to be\n497\tknown in advance. In practice, speaker count estimation errors are\n498\tunavoidable when C is unknown. Although speaker counting is\n499\tbeyond the scope of this work, we analyze the effect of estimation\n500\t\n501\t\fFig. 3: Permutation accuracy as a function of estimated speaker\n502\tcount Ĉ on the 4-speaker sparse long speech test set (segment\n503\tlength=8 s, segment overlap=25%).\n504\terrors by sweeping the estimated speaker count Ĉ ∈ {3, 4, 5, 6} on\n505\tthe 4-speaker sparse long speech test set using the 3-output separator, covering cases where the speaker count is correctly estimated,\n506\tunderestimated, and overestimated.\n507\tCSS-based methods are excluded from this comparison, as they\n508\taim to minimize overlap in output streams rather than performing\n509\tglobal permutation alignment, and are therefore not dependent on\n510\tĈ. For Sortformer (AOSC), we report only Ĉ = 3 and Ĉ = 4,\n511\tsince the released model supports at most four outputs. For baseline\n512\tmethods, each is run with C = Ĉ following the same procedure as\n513\tin the C-known experiments. The proposed dynamic clustering is\n514\tprimarily developed for the case where the number of speakers is\n515\tknown and equals the number of separator outputs. When Ĉ > 3,\n516\tthe assumption no longer holds, and a warm-up initialization stage\n517\tis introduced to handle this mismatch. Embeddings from the first 10\n518\tsegments are collected and clustered into Ĉ groups via k-means to\n519\tinitialize the Ĉ reference pools. Each segment’s 3 separator outputs\n520\tare then matched to the reference pools and assigned to 3 of the Ĉ\n521\toutput streams, producing Ĉ complete output streams for the full\n522\trecording. Since the permutation accuracy in Eq. (5) assumes Ĉ =\n523\tC, we adopt a modified permutation accuracy for this experiment\n524\twhere a global optimal matching between the Ĉ estimated streams\n525\tand the C true speakers is first established across the full recording,\n526\tand a segment is counted as correct if all its separator outputs are\n527\tassigned to the matched speakers under this global mapping.\n528\tFig. 3 shows the permutation accuracy as a function of Ĉ.\n529\tAcross all methods, underestimation causes larger performance\n530\tdegradation than overestimation, as assigning fewer streams than\n531\tthe true speaker count forces multiple speakers to share the same\n532\toutput, making correct permutation alignment fundamentally impossible for the merged speakers. In contrast, overestimation introduces\n533\tadditional streams, while correct alignment of the existing speakers remains achievable. Unlike other methods whose performance\n534\tdecreases under overestimation, UPGMC clustering shows a consistent performance increase with larger Ĉ, suggesting that additional\n535\tstreams provide more flexibility for separating acoustically similar\n536\tspeaker embeddings during global clustering. Compared to all baselines, the proposed method maintains the highest accuracy across\n537\tall Ĉ values, with a peak of 94.7% at the correct estimate Ĉ = 4.\n538\tThis consistent advantage suggests that the quality-weighted mechanism remains effective in scenarios where the number of speakers\n539\tis unknown, even when the speaker count is incorrectly estimated.\n540\t\n541\tFig. 4: t-SNE visualization of speaker embeddings after permutation assignment by the proposed dynamic clustering, on a 4-speaker\n542\tsparse long speech sample with Ĉ = 5. Colors indicate assigned\n543\toutput streams, marker shapes indicate the true speaker identity, and\n544\tmarker size and opacity reflect the quality score of each embedding.\n545\tStar markers denote embeddings retained in the reference pools.\n546\t5.5. Embedding-Space Visualization of Dynamic Clustering\n547\tTo further examine the behavior of the proposed method under\n548\tspeaker count mismatch, Fig. 4 visualizes the speaker embedding\n549\tdistributions using t-SNE [27] for a randomly selected sample from\n550\tthe 4-speaker test set with Ĉ = 5, following the same experimental\n551\tsetup as Section 5.4.\n552\tThe embeddings form five clusters in the t-SNE space, with each\n553\tcluster corresponding to one output stream. Within each cluster, embeddings from the same true speaker dominate, confirming that the\n554\tproposed method correctly assigns embeddings to their corresponding output streams. The cluster corresponding to Stream 1 contains\n555\tsignificantly fewer embeddings than the others, with the majority\n556\tbeing star markers, suggesting that most embeddings in this cluster\n557\twere assigned during reference pool initialization with few subsequent additions, which explains the high permutation accuracy of\n558\t91.5% achieved by the proposed method at Ĉ = 5 in Section 5.4.\n559\tMost star markers corresponding to Stream 2–5 are concentrated\n560\tnear the center region of each cluster, confirming that the proposed\n561\treference pool strategy successfully retains the highly representative\n562\tembeddings for each speaker even under speaker count mismatch.\n563\t6. CONCLUSIONS\n564\tThis paper addressed the cross-segment permutation problem in long\n565\tspeech separation by proposing a dynamic clustering approach built\n566\tupon the speaker cache strategy. The method maintains dynamic reference pools of high-quality speaker embeddings and makes permutation decisions based on weighted cosine similarity, requiring no additional training and functioning as a plug-and-play post-processing\n567\tmodule compatible with existing separation models. Experimental\n568\tresults confirm that dynamic clustering achieves robust permutation\n569\taccuracy and maintains speaker consistency across output streams\n570\teven with extended utterance gaps, which is essential for human listening applications. Experiments in the unknown speaker count scenario further demonstrate that the proposed method maintains reliable permutation accuracy and a consistent advantage over baselines\n571\teven when the speaker count is incorrectly estimated.\n572\t\n573\t\f7. REFERENCES\n574\t[1] D. Wang and J. Chen, “Supervised speech separation based\n575\ton deep learning: An overview,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio,\n576\tSpeech, Lang. Process., vol. 26, no. 10, pp. 1702–1726, 2018.\n577\t[2] E. Vincent, T. Virtanen, and S. Gannot, Audio Source Separation and Speech Enhancement, John Wiley & Sons, 2018.\n578\t[3] Shoko Araki, Nobutaka Ito, Reinhold Haeb-Umbach, Gordon\n579\tWichern, Zhong-Qiu Wang, and Yuki Mitsufuji, “30+ years\n580\tof source separation research: Achievements and future challenges,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n581\t[4] Yi Luo and Nima Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal\n582\ttime–frequency magnitude masking for speech separation,”\n583\tIEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol. 27, no.\n584\t8, pp. 1256–1266, 2019.\n585\t[5] Dong Yu, Morten Kolbæk, Zheng-Hua Tan, and Jesper Jensen,\n586\t“Permutation invariant training of deep models for speakerindependent multi-talker speech separation,” in Proc. IEEE\n587\tInt. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process., 2017, pp. 241–245.\n588\t[6] Morten Kolbæk, Dong Yu, Zheng-Hua Tan, and Jesper Jensen,\n589\t“Multitalker speech separation with utterance-level permutation invariant training of deep recurrent neural networks,”\n590\tIEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol. 25, no.\n591\t10, pp. 1901–1913, 2017.\n592\t[7] Zhong-Qiu Wang, Samuele Cornell, Shukjae Choi, Younglo\n593\tLee, Byeong-Yeol Kim, and Shinji Watanabe, “TF-GridNet:\n594\tIntegrating full- and sub-band modeling for speech separation,”\n595\tIEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol. 31, pp.\n596\t3221–3236, 2023.\n597\t[8] Yi Luo, Zhuo Chen, and Takuya Yoshioka, “Dual-Path RNN:\n598\tEfficient long sequence modeling for time-domain singlechannel speech separation,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust.,\n599\tSpeech Signal Process., 2020, pp. 46–50.\n600\t[9] Chenda Li, Zhuo Chen, Yi Luo, Cong Han, Tianyan Zhou,\n601\tKeisuke Kinoshita, Marc Delcroix, Shinji Watanabe, and Yanmin Qian, “Dual-path modeling for long recording speech separation in meetings,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech\n602\tSignal Process. IEEE, 2021, pp. 5739–5743.\n603\t[10] Yuzhu Wang, Archontis Politis, Konstantinos Drossos, and\n604\tTuomas Virtanen, “Multi-utterance speech separation and association trained on short segments,” in Proc. IEEE WASPAA.\n605\tIEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n606\t[11] Yuzhu Wang, Archontis Politis, Konstantinos Drossos, and\n607\tTuomas Virtanen, “Attractor-based speech separation of multiple utterances by unknown number of speakers,” in Proc.\n608\tInterspeech. IEEE, 2025, pp. 1453–1457.\n609\t[12] Zhuo Chen, Takuya Yoshioka, Liang Lu, Tianyan Zhou, Zhong\n610\tMeng, Yi Luo, Jian Wu, Xiong Xiao, and Jinyu Li, “Continuous speech separation: Dataset and analysis,” in Proc.\n611\tIEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2020,\n612\tpp. 7284–7288.\n613\t[13] Hervé Bredin,\n614\t“pyannote. audio 2.1 speaker diarization\n615\tpipeline: principle, benchmark, and recipe,” in Proc. Interspeech. ISCA, 2023, pp. 1983–1987.\n616\t[14] Thilo Von Neumann, Christoph Boeddeker, Tobias CordLandwehr, Marc Delcroix, and Reinhold Haeb-Umbach,\n617\t“Meeting recognition with continuous speech separation and\n618\t\n619\ttranscription-supported diarization,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf.\n620\tAcoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2024, pp. 775–779.\n621\t[15] Cong Han, Yi Luo, Chenda Li, Tianyan Zhou, Keisuke Kinoshita, Shinji Watanabe, Marc Delcroix, Hakan Erdogan,\n622\tJohn R Hershey, Nima Mesgarani, et al., “Continuous speech\n623\tseparation using speaker inventory for long recording,” in\n624\tProc. Interspeech, 2021, pp. 3036–3040.\n625\t[16] Yawen Xue, Shota Horiguchi, Yusuke Fujita, Shinji Watanabe,\n626\tPaola Garcı́a, and Kenji Nagamatsu, “Online end-to-end neural\n627\tdiarization with speaker-tracing buffer,” in Proc. SLT. IEEE,\n628\t2021, pp. 841–848.\n629\t[17] Ivan Medennikov, Taejin Park, Weiqing Wang, He Huang, Kunal Dhawan, Jinhan Wang, Jagadeesh Balam, and Boris Ginsburg, “Streaming sortformer: Speaker cache-based online\n630\tspeaker diarization with arrival-time ordering,” in Proc. Interspeech. IEEE, 2025, pp. 5238–5242.\n631\t[18] Shota Horiguchi, Shinji Watanabe, Paola Garcı́a, Yuki\n632\tTakashima, and Yohei Kawaguchi, “Online neural diarization\n633\tof unlimited numbers of speakers using global and local attractors,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol.\n634\t31, pp. 706–720, 2022.\n635\t[19] Zhengyang Chen, Bing Han, Shuai Wang, and Yanmin Qian,\n636\t“Attention-based encoder-decoder end-to-end neural diarization with embedding enhancer,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio,\n637\tSpeech, Lang. Process., vol. 32, pp. 1636–1649, 2024.\n638\t[20] David Snyder, Daniel Garcia-Romero, Gregory Sell, Daniel\n639\tPovey, and Sanjeev Khudanpur, “X-vectors: Robust dnn embeddings for speaker recognition,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf.\n640\tAcoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2018, pp. 5329–5333.\n641\t[21] Brecht Desplanques, Jenthe Thienpondt, and Kris Demuynck,\n642\t“ECAPA-TDNN: Emphasized channel attention, propagation\n643\tand aggregation in TDNN based speaker verification,” in Proc.\n644\tInterspeech. IEEE, 2020, pp. 3830–3834.\n645\t[22] Vassil Panayotov, Guoguo Chen, Daniel Povey, and Sanjeev\n646\tKhudanpur, “LibriSpeech: an ASR corpus based on public\n647\tdomain audio books,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech\n648\tSignal Process., 2015.\n649\t[23] Jonathan Le Roux, Scott Wisdom, Hakan Erdogan, and John R.\n650\tHershey, “SDR – half-baked or well done?,” in Proc. IEEE Int.\n651\tConf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process., 2019, pp. 626–630.\n652\t[24] Joonas Kalda, Ricard Marxer, Tanel Alumäe, and Hervé\n653\tBredin, “Pixit: Joint training of speaker diarization and speech\n654\tseparation from real-world multi-speaker recordings,” in Proc.\n655\tInterspeech. IEEE, 2024, pp. 115–122.\n656\t[25] Alexis Plaquet and Hervé Bredin, “Powerset multi-class cross\n657\tentropy loss for neural speaker diarization,” in Proc. Interspeech. IEEE, 2023, pp. 3222–3226.\n658\t[26] Arsha Nagrani, Joon Son Chung, and Andrew Zisserman,\n659\t“Voxceleb: a large-scale speaker identification dataset,” in\n660\tProc. Interspeech. IEEE, 2017, pp. 2616–2620.\n661\t[27] Laurens Van der Maaten and Geoffrey Hinton, “Visualizing\n662\tdata using t-SNE,” Journal of Machine Learning Research,\n663\tvol. 9, no. 11, 2008.\n664\t\n665\t\f\n666\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "624ba7d8-45d5-4825-9fbf-10553d6a6676", "uuid": "9cce0e71-a034-4e7c-b8e1-105b22533459", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T16:24:54.952Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/624ba7d8-45d5-4825-9fbf-10553d6a6676/tool-results/bqdrzprnm.txt", "content": "2026 IEEE INTERNATIONAL WORKSHOP ON MACHINE LEARNING FOR SIGNAL PROCESSING, SEP. 28– OCT. 1, 2026, ATLANTA, USA\n\nDYNAMIC CLUSTERING FOR CROSS-SEGMENT PERMUTATION ALIGNMENT\nIN LONG SPEECH SEPARATION\nYuzhu Wang1 , Archontis Politis1 , Konstantinos Drossos2 , Tuomas Virtanen1\n1\n\nSignal Processing Research Centre, Tampere University, Tampere, Finland\n2\nNokia Technologies, Espoo, Finland\n\narXiv:2608.09451v1 [eess.AS] 10 Aug 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nLong speech separation typically employs a segment-separationstitch paradigm where recordings are divided into short segments,\nprocessed independently, and stitched together. Its challenge lies\nin predicting cross-segment permutations. This paper proposes a\ntraining-free dynamic clustering approach for cross-segment permutation alignment using speaker embedding reference pools. The\nmethod predicts the permutation using the cosine similarity between\ncurrent segment embeddings and the reference pools. The approach\nupdates reference pools by retaining the most representative speaker\nembeddings based on their overall cosine similarity with existing\nreferences. As a plug-and-play post-processing module compatible\nwith existing separation models, the proposed method demonstrates\nsuperior performance compared to existing methods on dense and\nsparse long speech scenarios, particularly in challenging sparse scenarios with extended utterance gaps, and further shows robustness to\nspeaker count estimation errors in unknown speaker count scenarios.\nIndex Terms— Speech separation, permutation, clustering,\nspeaker embeddings, long speech processing\n1. INTRODUCTION\nSpeech separation is the task of isolating individual speaker signals\nfrom mixtures. Deep neural network (DNN) based methods have\nbecome mainstream [1–3]. Most current research focuses on separation models operating on short segments, typically ranging from\na few seconds to tens of seconds [4–7]. However, practical applications often involve arbitrarily long recordings or continuously\nstreamed audio such as meetings, lectures, and broadcasts.\nSeparating arbitrarily long recordings can be achieved by enhancing the generalization capability of separation models [8–11],\nbut such methods typically generalize only to recording lengths of\nseveral minutes before facing computational complexity explosion\nand performance degradation. A more scalable alternative involves\nsegmenting long recordings into shorter segments during inference,\nperforming separation on each segment independently, and then\nstitching the results into complete outputs [12–15]. Theoretically,\nthis paradigm can handle arbitrarily long recordings. Since separation models are typically speaker-independent and trained using\npermutation invariant training (PIT) [5], the assignment of speakers to outputs is arbitrary and inconsistent between segments, and\nthe performance of such approaches is therefore dominated by the\naccuracy of cross-segment permutation assignment.\nExisting solutions to the cross-segment permutation problem\ncan be categorized into three types. The first approach is overlap matching, as used in continuous speech separation (CSS) [12],\nwhich determines the permutation of each segment by matching the\n\noverlapping region between adjacent segments. This approach is\ncomputationally efficient and requires no speaker embeddings, but\nonly ensures local utterance continuity within each output stream\nwithout resolving the global permutation across the full recording. The second approach applies global clustering to speaker\nembeddings extracted from separated outputs [13]. The standard\npipeline extracts speaker embeddings from voice activity detection\n(VAD)-segmented speech regions and groups them using clustering\nalgorithms such as k-means, spectral clustering, or agglomerative\nclustering, assigning speaker labels globally across all segments.\nHowever, global clustering is limited to offline processing. Residual\ninterference in separated outputs increases the risk of VAD errors\nthat introduce unreliable embeddings, which can distort clustering results by shifting centroids or causing misassignment across\nspeaker clusters, with the severity depending on the robustness of\nthe chosen clustering algorithm [14]. The third approach maintains\na speaker cache to guide permutation alignment. Static cache methods, such as speaker inventory [15], pre-detect single-active-speaker\nregions from the mixture to build a fixed speaker reference that is\nused without further updates. Dynamic speaker cache methods,\nas employed in streaming speaker diarization such as the speakertracing buffer (STB) [16] and more recently arrival-order speaker\ncache (AOSC) [17], maintain a set of continuously updated speaker\nrepresentations for streaming identity tracking, proven effective in\nspeaker diarization.\nAll three approaches are used in meeting processing systems\nwhere the primary goal is automatic speech recognition (ASR) and\ntranscription. In such systems, cross-segment permutation consistency receives limited attention, as individual utterances are recognized independently after VAD segmentation, and utterance-level\nalignment is sufficient to produce accurate recognition results. In\ncontrast, human listening applications such as hearing aids, multiparty call recording, and interview production require consistent\nspeaker identity across multiple utterances. Permutation errors\ncause different speakers’ voices to alternate within the same output\nchannel, severely degrading the perceptual quality. Dedicated study\nof cross-segment permutation alignment for separated audio is lacking, particularly for sparse speech scenarios with extended utterance\ngaps.\nTo address the cross-segment permutation problem in long\nspeech separation, we propose a dynamic clustering approach built\nupon the speaker cache strategy, as shown in Fig. 1. Similar to\ndynamic cache methods in streaming speaker diarization [16, 17],\nthe proposed method maintains a reference pool for each speaker\nand resolves cross-segment permutations by matching current segment embeddings against cached historical representations. However, speaker diarization systems typically operate on mixture signals and output temporal speaker activity labels targeting ASR\n\n\fpipelines [18, 19], whereas the proposed method operates directly\non separated outputs to produce speaker-consistent audio streams.\nFurthermore, recent dynamic cache methods rely on neural network\nprediction scores for cache updates [16, 17], making their generalization performance dependent on the match between training data\nand the target scenario. In contrast, since separation quality varies\nacross segments and low-quality embeddings from poorly separated\nregions are unavoidable, the proposed method measures embedding reliability through inter-embedding weighted cosine similarity\nand retains only the most representative embeddings in the reference pool via a TopN update strategy, naturally suppressing lowquality embeddings without relying on VAD. The proposed method\nrequires no additional training and functions as a plug-and-play\npost-processing module compatible with existing separation models. Evaluation on dense and sparse long speech scenarios shows\nthat the proposed method achieves robust performance compared to\nexisting approaches, with particularly substantial improvements in\nsparse scenarios. The proposed method also exhibits robustness to\nspeaker count estimation errors, maintaining superior performance\nover baselines even when the speaker count is incorrectly estimated.\n2. PROBLEM FORMULATION\nP\nConsider a long-duration mixture signal y(t) = C\nc=1 xc (t) + n(t)\ncontaining speech from C unknown speakers and noise n(t), where\nxc (t) consists of multiple utterances separated by silence intervals. The mixture is divided into S segments of length L with\nhop size H (0 < H ≤ L). Each segment ys (t) is processed\nby a speaker-independent separation model to obtain C outputs\n{x̂s,1 (t), . . . , x̂s,C (t)}. Since speaker identities are unknown, the\nassignment of speakers and separated outputs is arbitrary and inconsistent between segments. The unknown permutation for segment s\nis denoted as πs ∈ ΠC , where ΠC denotes the set of all permutations of {1, 2, . . . , C}, and πs (c) indicates which speaker the c-th\nseparated output corresponds to. The cross-segment permutation\nproblem aims to predict the sequence of permutations {π1∗ , . . . , πS∗ }\nsuch that separated segments from the same speaker are consistently\nassigned to the same output index across all segments. The number\nof active speakers and utterances in each segment ys (t) is unknown\nand variable.\n3. PROPOSED METHOD\nSeparation quality varies across segments for long recordings, and\nsegments where speakers are insufficiently separated or where residual noise is prominent easily yield low-quality embeddings. As illustrated in Fig. 1, the proposed method maintains a separate reference pool for each speaker and retains only the most representative\nembeddings via a TopN selection strategy. Since high cosine similarity scores can only arise from embeddings belonging to the same\nspeaker, the reference pool naturally converges toward high-quality\nsame-speaker embeddings over time while progressively displacing\nunreliable ones, remaining robust to noise and capable of accumulating representative embeddings across arbitrarily long recordings.\n\nFig. 1: Dynamic clustering for cross-segment permutation: (a) long\nspeech separation workflow, (b) quality-weighted permutation assignment using reference pools, (c) reference pool updates based on\nsegment quality assessment.\nat least one active speaker in the first N segments. This assumption is easily satisfied in practice, as leading silence regions can\nbe removed by simple preprocessing before processing. For the\nfirst segment, we use identity permutation where π1 (c) = c for all\nc, and the embeddings {e1,1 , . . . , e1,C } are added to their corresponding reference pools with quality score initialized to 0.5. For\neach segment s > 1, the permutation is determined by finding the\nassignment that maximizes the quality-weighted similarity between\ncurrent separated output embeddings and reference embeddings in\nthe reference pools,\nπs∗ = arg max\n\nπ∈ΠC\n\nC\nX\n\nX\n\nqj · sim(es,π(c) , eref\nj ),\n\n(1)\n\nc=1 (eref ,qj )∈Rs−1,c\nj\n\nwhere sim(·, ·) denotes cosine similarity, which is widely used for\ncomparing speaker embeddings due to its effectiveness in capturing angular relationships between high-dimensional vectors [20,21].\nThe quality scores qj are used to emphasize representative embeddings over less characteristic ones in Eq. (1).\n3.2. Quality Assessment\nThe quality score qs for segment s is computed as the weighted cosine similarity between all speakers’ embeddings and their corresponding reference pools as\nqs =\n\nC\nX\n1\nC · |Rs−1,c | c=1\n\nX\n\nqj · sim(e∗s,c , eref\nj ),\n\n(2)\n\n(eref\nj ,qj )∈Rs−1,c\n\n3.1. Quality-Driven Dynamic Clustering\nWe extract speaker embeddings {es,1 , . . . , es,C } from separated\noutputs of segment s using a pretrained speaker embedding model,\nas shown in Fig. 1(b). For each segment s, there are C separate\ndynamic reference pools Rs,c , where each pool contains up to N\nref\ntuples (eref\nj , qj ) of embeddings ej and their corresponding quality\nscores qj from previously processed segments. The method assumes\n\nwhere e∗s,c is the embedding extracted from the separated output assigned to speaker c using predicted πs∗ from Eq. (1).\n3.3. Reference Pool Management\nAs shown in Fig. 1(c), the reference pools are updated by retaining\nthe top N highest-quality embeddings from the union of the current\n\n\fembeddings and existing reference pool embeddings,\n(\nRs−1,c ∪ {(e∗s,c , qs )}\nif |Rs−1,c | < N\nRs,c =\nTopN(Rs−1,c ∪ {(e∗s,c , qs )}) otherwise\n\n(3)\n\nwhere N is the maximum pool size, the quality score qs is from\nEq. (2), and TopN(·) selects the N highest-score embeddings.\n4. EXPERIMENTAL SETTINGS\n4.1. Datasets\nWe generate long speech test sets with 2 to 4 speakers to evaluate permutation alignment under varying conditions. The 2-speaker\ndense and sparse test sets follow the generation approach in [10]. For\neach mixture, we randomly select two speakers from LibriSpeech\ntrain-clean-360 [22] and concatenate all utterances for each speaker\nin random order. We use train-clean-360 rather than test-clean due\nto the limited number of speakers and fewer utterances per speaker\nin test-clean, which are insufficient for generating long speech test\nsets. Random silence intervals are inserted before each utterance,\nwith durations uniformly sampled from [1, 10] seconds for the dense\ntest set and [10, 30] seconds for the sparse test set. The overlap rate\nis defined as the ratio of overlapping speech duration to the total\nduration containing active speech. Room acoustic simulation and\nreal-world noise addition follow the parameters as [10]. All signals\nare single-channel at 16 kHz sampling rate with no speaker overlap\nbetween training and test sets. Each test set contains 200 mixtures.\nThe dense test set achieves overlap rates from 31.2% to 69.7% with\nan average of 50.1%, resulting in a total duration of 121 hours with\nmixture lengths averaging 36 minutes (range: 34-38 minutes). The\nsparse test set achieves overlap rates from 8.5% to 29.4% with an\naverage of 19.8%, resulting in a total duration of 213 hours with\nmixture lengths averaging 64 minutes (range: 54-76 minutes).\nWe generate 3-speaker and 4-speaker sparse long speech test sets\nfollowing the same simulation procedure and parameter settings as\nthe 2-speaker case, with at most 3 speakers active simultaneously,\nfollowing the setup in [12].\n4.2. Evaluation Metrics\nWe evaluate the permutation solutions using SI-SDR [23] and\nsegment-wise permutation accuracy. SI-SDR is computed on the\nentire long speech signal after stitching all segments, reflecting both\nwithin-segment separation quality and cross-segment permutation\nconsistency. We determine oracle permutations for each segment\nbetween separated outputs x̂ and ground truth reference signals xref\nas\nC\nX\nπsoracle = arg min\nLSI-SDR (x̂s,π(c) , xref\n(4)\ns,c ).\nπ∈ΠC\n\nc=1\n\nFrom the accuracy calculation, we exclude segments where all reference signals are silent using a root mean square (RMS) energy\nthreshold of −30 dB, as speaker permutations are undefined in these\nsegments. The segment-wise permutation accuracy is computed as\nX\n1\n1[πs∗ = πsoracle ],\n(5)\nAccuracy =\n|Svalid | s∈S\nvalid\n\nwhere Svalid denotes the set of non-silent segments, πs∗ is the predicted permutation, and 1[·] is the indicator function that equals 1\nwhen the condition is true and 0 otherwise. To account for potential global speaker assignment ambiguity, we evaluate all possible\nglobal permutations of speaker assignments and select the one with\nthe highest accuracy.\n\nTable 1: Performance comparison of different systems using 8 s\nsegments with 25% overlap. Dense scenario: overlap rate 50.1%,\nunprocessed mixture SI-SDR −1.4 dB. Sparse scenario: overlap\nrate 19.8%, unprocessed mixture SI-SDR −1.2 dB. Values show\nsegment-wise permutation accuracy(%) / SI-SDR(dB).\nSystem\n\nDense\n\nSparse\n\nOracle\nCSS-sep [12]\nCSS-sep + VAD + emb. + k-clustering [14]\nSep + speaker inventory [15]\nSortformer (AOSC) [17]\nSep + VAD + emb. + UPGMC-clustering [13]\n\n–/11.8\n59.1/-3.0\n97.1/11.2\n85.0/5.7\n85.2/5.9\n91.3/9.2\n\n–/14.3\n53.7/-4.5\n89.3/8.2\n74.1/3.5\n71.1/2.7\n71.6/2.8\n\nSep + emb. + dynamic clustering (proposed)\n\n98.5/11.7\n\n96.8/13.3\n\n4.3. Baselines and Pretrained Models\nWe compare the proposed method against five baselines. CSSsep [12] stitches separated segments by matching overlapping regions between adjacent segments. CSS-sep + VAD + embedding +\nk-clustering [14] applies VAD to CSS outputs, extracts speaker embeddings from speech-active regions, and reassigns output streams\nvia global k-means clustering. Sep + speaker inventory [15] identifies single-speaker regions to build a fixed speaker reference,\nwhich is established once and unchanged throughout the recording\nfor permutation guidance. Sep + VAD + embedding + UPGMC\nclustering [13] extracts speaker embeddings from VAD-segmented\nspeech regions of each separated segment and groups them using\nan agglomerative hierarchical clustering with centroid linkage (UPGMC algorithm). Sortformer (AOSC) [17] is an end-to-end online\nneural diarization system. We use the released model1 and run it\non the mixture to obtain global speaker diarization labels, which\nare then converted to segment-wise permutations for cross-segment\nalignment of the separated outputs following [24]. The model was\ntrained on 2445 hours of real conversations and 5150 hours of simulated mixtures including LibriSpeech, which potentially overlaps\nwith the test sets used in this work.\nAll methods use the same FTRNN separator trained following the original implementation [10], with a 2-output version for\n2-speaker experiments and a 3-output version for 3-speaker and 4speaker experiments. Methods requiring VAD employ a pre-trained\npyannote voice activity detection model2 [25]. For methods requiring speaker embeddings, we employ a pre-trained ECAPA-TDNN\nmodel3 [21] trained on VoxCeleb [26]. Speaker inventory employs\na pre-trained speaker diarization model4 [13] to identify singlespeaker segments. All pretrained models operate in a zero-shot\nmanner, as their training data does not overlap with the separation\nmodel’s training data. Dynamic clustering uses a reference pool size\nof N = 10, where the sensitivity to this parameter is examined in\nthe ablation study.\n5. RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS\n5.1. Overall Performance\nTable 1 presents the overall performance of all methods on the 2speaker dense and sparse long speech scenarios. CSS-sep achieves\n59.1% and 53.7% permutation accuracy on the dense and sparse\nscenarios respectively. It is worth noting that CSS is not designed\n1 huggingface.co/nvidia/diar streaming sortformer 4spk-v2\n2 huggingface.co/pyannote/voice-activity-detection\n3 huggingface.co/speechbrain/spkrec-ecapa-voxceleb\n4 huggingface.co/pyannote/speaker-diarization-3.1\n\n\fFig. 2: Permutation accuracy on the sparse long speech scenario as a function of (a) segment length (segment overlap=25%, 2 speakers), (b)\nsegment overlap (segment length=8 s, 2 speakers), and (c) number of speakers (segment length=8 s, segment overlap=25%).\nfor global permutation alignment but rather aims to minimize overlap in output streams. CSS-sep + VAD + embedding + k-clustering\nperforms substantially better in the dense scenario (97.1%), building on the local utterance continuity provided by CSS. In the sparse\nscenario, performance drops to 89.3%, as the CSS stitching stage becomes less reliable under longer utterance gaps and the higher proportion of silence increases the risk of VAD false detections. Sep\n+ speaker inventory achieves 85.0% and 74.1% on the dense and\nsparse scenarios, showing consistent but moderate performance in\nboth cases. Sortformer (AOSC) achieves 85.2% and 71.1% on the\ndense and sparse scenarios respectively. Despite potential speaker\noverlap between its training data and our test sets, the performance\nremains moderate, suggesting that the generalization of end-to-end\ndiarization systems is sensitive to mismatches in acoustic conditions such as noise, reverberation, and overlap rate between training\nand test scenarios. Sep + VAD + embedding + UPGMC clustering\nachieves 91.3% in the dense scenario, lower than CSS + VAD + embedding + k-clustering. In the sparse scenario, performance drops\nmore severely to 71.6%, possibly due to increased VAD false detections under high silence proportions, which introduces unreliable\nembeddings into the clustering process.\nThe proposed method achieves 98.5%/11.7 dB in the dense scenario and 96.8%/13.3 dB in the sparse scenario, respectively. The\nSI-SDR of 11.7 dB in the dense scenario is very close to the oracle\nof 11.8 dB, indicating that the proposed method approaches the upper bound of achievable performance. The higher oracle SI-SDR in\nthe sparse scenario (14.3 dB vs. 11.8 dB) reflects that lower overlap\nrates make within-segment separation inherently easier. Across all\nmethods, the larger performance gap from oracle in the sparse scenario confirms that permutation accuracy is the primary bottleneck.\n\n5.2. Analysis on Sparse Scenario\nAs shown in Fig. 2(a), all methods show relatively stable performance across segment lengths in the 2-speaker sparse scenario, with\nminor improvements for longer segments. As shown in Fig. 2(b),\nCSS-sep and CSS-sep + VAD + embedding + k-clustering require\nnon-zero overlap and are therefore not applicable at 0% overlap,\nwhile the remaining methods show stable performance across overlap rates. As shown in Fig. 2(c), all methods experience performance\ndegradation when the number of speakers increases from 2 to 3,\nreflecting the increased permutation complexity with more speakers. CSS-sep shows the most severe degradation, approaching near-\n\nTable 2: Ablation study on dynamic clustering components using\n8 s segments with 25% overlap in sparse scenario. Values show\nsegment-wise permutation accuracy(%)/SI-SDR(dB).\n#\n\nQuality Weighting\nin Eq. (1)\n\nQuality Weighting\nin Eq. (2)\n\nSimilarity\n\nN\n\nInit.\nScore\n\nAcc/SI-SDR\n\n1\n2\n3\n4\n\n✓\n×\n✓\n×\n\n✓\n✓\n×\n×\n\nCosine\nCosine\nCosine\nCosine\n\n10\n10\n10\n10\n\n0.5\n0.5\n0.5\n0.5\n\n96.8/13.3\n95.8/12.7\n90.8/10.6\n90.6/10.5\n\n5\n6\n\n✓\n✓\n\n✓\n✓\n\nCosine\nCosine\n\n5\n2\n\n0.5\n0.5\n\n96.3/13.0\n96.3/12.9\n\n7\n8\n\n✓\n✓\n\n✓\n✓\n\nCosine\nCosine\n\n10\n10\n\n0.25\n0.75\n\n96.8/13.3\n96.8/13.3\n\n9\n\n✓\n\n✓\n\nEuclidean\n\n10\n\n0.5\n\n68.2/1.5\n\nrandom performance. The proposed method achieves the smallest\nrelative degradation and maintains the highest accuracy among all\nmethods. For scenarios with more speakers, the separator output\ncount is typically determined by the maximum number of simultaneously active speakers rather than the total speaker count [12], and\nresults under such settings are evaluated in Section 5.4.\n5.3. Ablation Study\nTable 2 presents an ablation study on the 2-speaker sparse scenario\nexamining the contribution of each component in dynamic clustering. Experiments 1-4 demonstrate the importance of quality weighting mechanisms. Removing quality weighting from permutation\ndecisions (experiment 2) reduces accuracy by 1.0%, while removing quality weighting in pool updates (experiment 3) causes severe\ndegradation of 6.0%. The method exhibits robustness to various parameter settings. Reference pool size variations (experiments 5-6)\nshow minimal performance impact, with N = 5 and N = 2 achieving comparable results to N = 10. Initial quality score settings\n(experiments 7-8) have negligible effect on final performance. Similarity metric choice proves critical, as replacing cosine similarity\nwith Euclidean distance (experiment 9) causes severe performance\ndegradation to 68.2% accuracy.\n5.4. Robustness to Speaker Count Mismatch\nAll experiments above assume the number of speakers C to be\nknown in advance. In practice, speaker count estimation errors are\nunavoidable when C is unknown. Although speaker counting is\nbeyond the scope of this work, we analyze the effect of estimation\n\n\fFig. 3: Permutation accuracy as a function of estimated speaker\ncount Ĉ on the 4-speaker sparse long speech test set (segment\nlength=8 s, segment overlap=25%).\nerrors by sweeping the estimated speaker count Ĉ ∈ {3, 4, 5, 6} on\nthe 4-speaker sparse long speech test set using the 3-output separator, covering cases where the speaker count is correctly estimated,\nunderestimated, and overestimated.\nCSS-based methods are excluded from this comparison, as they\naim to minimize overlap in output streams rather than performing\nglobal permutation alignment, and are therefore not dependent on\nĈ. For Sortformer (AOSC), we report only Ĉ = 3 and Ĉ = 4,\nsince the released model supports at most four outputs. For baseline\nmethods, each is run with C = Ĉ following the same procedure as\nin the C-known experiments. The proposed dynamic clustering is\nprimarily developed for the case where the number of speakers is\nknown and equals the number of separator outputs. When Ĉ > 3,\nthe assumption no longer holds, and a warm-up initialization stage\nis introduced to handle this mismatch. Embeddings from the first 10\nsegments are collected and clustered into Ĉ groups via k-means to\ninitialize the Ĉ reference pools. Each segment’s 3 separator outputs\nare then matched to the reference pools and assigned to 3 of the Ĉ\noutput streams, producing Ĉ complete output streams for the full\nrecording. Since the permutation accuracy in Eq. (5) assumes Ĉ =\nC, we adopt a modified permutation accuracy for this experiment\nwhere a global optimal matching between the Ĉ estimated streams\nand the C true speakers is first established across the full recording,\nand a segment is counted as correct if all its separator outputs are\nassigned to the matched speakers under this global mapping.\nFig. 3 shows the permutation accuracy as a function of Ĉ.\nAcross all methods, underestimation causes larger performance\ndegradation than overestimation, as assigning fewer streams than\nthe true speaker count forces multiple speakers to share the same\noutput, making correct permutation alignment fundamentally impossible for the merged speakers. In contrast, overestimation introduces\nadditional streams, while correct alignment of the existing speakers remains achievable. Unlike other methods whose performance\ndecreases under overestimation, UPGMC clustering shows a consistent performance increase with larger Ĉ, suggesting that additional\nstreams provide more flexibility for separating acoustically similar\nspeaker embeddings during global clustering. Compared to all baselines, the proposed method maintains the highest accuracy across\nall Ĉ values, with a peak of 94.7% at the correct estimate Ĉ = 4.\nThis consistent advantage suggests that the quality-weighted mechanism remains effective in scenarios where the number of speakers\nis unknown, even when the speaker count is incorrectly estimated.\n\nFig. 4: t-SNE visualization of speaker embeddings after permutation assignment by the proposed dynamic clustering, on a 4-speaker\nsparse long speech sample with Ĉ = 5. Colors indicate assigned\noutput streams, marker shapes indicate the true speaker identity, and\nmarker size and opacity reflect the quality score of each embedding.\nStar markers denote embeddings retained in the reference pools.\n5.5. Embedding-Space Visualization of Dynamic Clustering\nTo further examine the behavior of the proposed method under\nspeaker count mismatch, Fig. 4 visualizes the speaker embedding\ndistributions using t-SNE [27] for a randomly selected sample from\nthe 4-speaker test set with Ĉ = 5, following the same experimental\nsetup as Section 5.4.\nThe embeddings form five clusters in the t-SNE space, with each\ncluster corresponding to one output stream. Within each cluster, embeddings from the same true speaker dominate, confirming that the\nproposed method correctly assigns embeddings to their corresponding output streams. The cluster corresponding to Stream 1 contains\nsignificantly fewer embeddings than the others, with the majority\nbeing star markers, suggesting that most embeddings in this cluster\nwere assigned during reference pool initialization with few subsequent additions, which explains the high permutation accuracy of\n91.5% achieved by the proposed method at Ĉ = 5 in Section 5.4.\nMost star markers corresponding to Stream 2–5 are concentrated\nnear the center region of each cluster, confirming that the proposed\nreference pool strategy successfully retains the highly representative\nembeddings for each speaker even under speaker count mismatch.\n6. CONCLUSIONS\nThis paper addressed the cross-segment permutation problem in long\nspeech separation by proposing a dynamic clustering approach built\nupon the speaker cache strategy. The method maintains dynamic reference pools of high-quality speaker embeddings and makes permutation decisions based on weighted cosine similarity, requiring no additional training and functioning as a plug-and-play post-processing\nmodule compatible with existing separation models. Experimental\nresults confirm that dynamic clustering achieves robust permutation\naccuracy and maintains speaker consistency across output streams\neven with extended utterance gaps, which is essential for human listening applications. Experiments in the unknown speaker count scenario further demonstrate that the proposed method maintains reliable permutation accuracy and a consistent advantage over baselines\neven when the speaker count is incorrectly estimated.\n\n\f7. REFERENCES\n[1] D. Wang and J. Chen, “Supervised speech separation based\non deep learning: An overview,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio,\nSpeech, Lang. Process., vol. 26, no. 10, pp. 1702–1726, 2018.\n[2] E. Vincent, T. Virtanen, and S. Gannot, Audio Source Separation and Speech Enhancement, John Wiley & Sons, 2018.\n[3] Shoko Araki, Nobutaka Ito, Reinhold Haeb-Umbach, Gordon\nWichern, Zhong-Qiu Wang, and Yuki Mitsufuji, “30+ years\nof source separation research: Achievements and future challenges,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n[4] Yi Luo and Nima Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal\ntime–frequency magnitude masking for speech separation,”\nIEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol. 27, no.\n8, pp. 1256–1266, 2019.\n[5] Dong Yu, Morten Kolbæk, Zheng-Hua Tan, and Jesper Jensen,\n“Permutation invariant training of deep models for speakerindependent multi-talker speech separation,” in Proc. IEEE\nInt. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process., 2017, pp. 241–245.\n[6] Morten Kolbæk, Dong Yu, Zheng-Hua Tan, and Jesper Jensen,\n“Multitalker speech separation with utterance-level permutation invariant training of deep recurrent neural networks,”\nIEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol. 25, no.\n10, pp. 1901–1913, 2017.\n[7] Zhong-Qiu Wang, Samuele Cornell, Shukjae Choi, Younglo\nLee, Byeong-Yeol Kim, and Shinji Watanabe, “TF-GridNet:\nIntegrating full- and sub-band modeling for speech separation,”\nIEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol. 31, pp.\n3221–3236, 2023.\n[8] Yi Luo, Zhuo Chen, and Takuya Yoshioka, “Dual-Path RNN:\nEfficient long sequence modeling for time-domain singlechannel speech separation,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust.,\nSpeech Signal Process., 2020, pp. 46–50.\n[9] Chenda Li, Zhuo Chen, Yi Luo, Cong Han, Tianyan Zhou,\nKeisuke Kinoshita, Marc Delcroix, Shinji Watanabe, and Yanmin Qian, “Dual-path modeling for long recording speech separation in meetings,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech\nSignal Process. IEEE, 2021, pp. 5739–5743.\n[10] Yuzhu Wang, Archontis Politis, Konstantinos Drossos, and\nTuomas Virtanen, “Multi-utterance speech separation and association trained on short segments,” in Proc. IEEE WASPAA.\nIEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n[11] Yuzhu Wang, Archontis Politis, Konstantinos Drossos, and\nTuomas Virtanen, “Attractor-based speech separation of multiple utterances by unknown number of speakers,” in Proc.\nInterspeech. IEEE, 2025, pp. 1453–1457.\n[12] Zhuo Chen, Takuya Yoshioka, Liang Lu, Tianyan Zhou, Zhong\nMeng, Yi Luo, Jian Wu, Xiong Xiao, and Jinyu Li, “Continuous speech separation: Dataset and analysis,” in Proc.\nIEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2020,\npp. 7284–7288.\n[13] Hervé Bredin,\n“pyannote. audio 2.1 speaker diarization\npipeline: principle, benchmark, and recipe,” in Proc. Interspeech. ISCA, 2023, pp. 1983–1987.\n[14] Thilo Von Neumann, Christoph Boeddeker, Tobias CordLandwehr, Marc Delcroix, and Reinhold Haeb-Umbach,\n“Meeting recognition with continuous speech separation and\n\ntranscription-supported diarization,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf.\nAcoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2024, pp. 775–779.\n[15] Cong Han, Yi Luo, Chenda Li, Tianyan Zhou, Keisuke Kinoshita, Shinji Watanabe, Marc Delcroix, Hakan Erdogan,\nJohn R Hershey, Nima Mesgarani, et al., “Continuous speech\nseparation using speaker inventory for long recording,” in\nProc. Interspeech, 2021, pp. 3036–3040.\n[16] Yawen Xue, Shota Horiguchi, Yusuke Fujita, Shinji Watanabe,\nPaola Garcı́a, and Kenji Nagamatsu, “Online end-to-end neural\ndiarization with speaker-tracing buffer,” in Proc. SLT. IEEE,\n2021, pp. 841–848.\n[17] Ivan Medennikov, Taejin Park, Weiqing Wang, He Huang, Kunal Dhawan, Jinhan Wang, Jagadeesh Balam, and Boris Ginsburg, “Streaming sortformer: Speaker cache-based online\nspeaker diarization with arrival-time ordering,” in Proc. Interspeech. IEEE, 2025, pp. 5238–5242.\n[18] Shota Horiguchi, Shinji Watanabe, Paola Garcı́a, Yuki\nTakashima, and Yohei Kawaguchi, “Online neural diarization\nof unlimited numbers of speakers using global and local attractors,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech, Lang. Process., vol.\n31, pp. 706–720, 2022.\n[19] Zhengyang Chen, Bing Han, Shuai Wang, and Yanmin Qian,\n“Attention-based encoder-decoder end-to-end neural diarization with embedding enhancer,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio,\nSpeech, Lang. Process., vol. 32, pp. 1636–1649, 2024.\n[20] David Snyder, Daniel Garcia-Romero, Gregory Sell, Daniel\nPovey, and Sanjeev Khudanpur, “X-vectors: Robust dnn embeddings for speaker recognition,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf.\nAcoust., Speech Signal Process. IEEE, 2018, pp. 5329–5333.\n[21] Brecht Desplanques, Jenthe Thienpondt, and Kris Demuynck,\n“ECAPA-TDNN: Emphasized channel attention, propagation\nand aggregation in TDNN based speaker verification,” in Proc.\nInterspeech. IEEE, 2020, pp. 3830–3834.\n[22] Vassil Panayotov, Guoguo Chen, Daniel Povey, and Sanjeev\nKhudanpur, “LibriSpeech: an ASR corpus based on public\ndomain audio books,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust., Speech\nSignal Process., 2015.\n[23] Jonathan Le Roux, Scott Wisdom, Hakan Erdogan, and John R.\nHershey, “SDR – half-baked or well done?,” in Proc. IEEE Int.\nConf. Acoust., Speech Signal Process., 2019, pp. 626–630.\n[24] Joonas Kalda, Ricard Marxer, Tanel Alumäe, and Hervé\nBredin, “Pixit: Joint training of speaker diarization and speech\nseparation from real-world multi-speaker recordings,” in Proc.\nInterspeech. IEEE, 2024, pp. 115–122.\n[25] Alexis Plaquet and Hervé Bredin, “Powerset multi-class cross\nentropy loss for neural speaker diarization,” in Proc. Interspeech. IEEE, 2023, pp. 3222–3226.\n[26] Arsha Nagrani, Joon Son Chung, and Andrew Zisserman,\n“Voxceleb: a large-scale speaker identification dataset,” in\nProc. Interspeech. IEEE, 2017, pp. 2616–2620.\n[27] Laurens Van der Maaten and Geoffrey Hinton, “Visualizing\ndata using t-SNE,” Journal of Machine Learning Research,\nvol. 9, no. 11, 2008.\n\n\f\n", "numLines": 666, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 666}}}
